The changes EveExtract makes to CUE4Parse (Apache-2.0). EveExtract.csproj applies them to the pinned submodule before it
builds; each changed part carries a notice, as section 4(b) of the Apache License 2.0 asks. Upstream still has all of
them at the pinned commit e4ea4ba8ec2b88d08b2066dfb8962863dccb36cc:

- ExportSession.ResolveOutputPath checks that every export path stays under the export folder before a folder is made
  or a file written: package paths and object names come from game files, which mods can change.
- ExportSession.ResolveOutputPath turned every slash into a backslash, which on Linux writes files whose names hold
  backslashes; only Windows gets them now.
- CUE4Parse.csproj no longer compiles Utils/CRC32.cs, DotNetZip's code under the MS-PL, which GPL-3.0 cannot take:
  tools/eve-extract/CRC32.cs (GPL-3.0-or-later) takes its place. It no longer embeds the resources of other games
  (Ace Combat 7's key, PUBG's and Borderlands 4's name maps, ProSpi's prebuilt decryptor DLL), which EveExtract never
  reads and which come without a licence it could pass on.
- CUE4Parse-Conversion.csproj no longer embeds the prebuilt Windows DLLs of Detex, tegra_swizzle and crunch: EveExtract
  decodes with AssetRipper.TextureDecoder on every OS and reads no Switch-swizzled or crunched texture.
- TextureEncoder encodes PNG with ImageSharp instead of SkiaSharp, through the same pixel conversion, so that
  EveExtract ships without SkiaSharp's native library: that library carries the Adobe DNG SDK, whose licence (an
  indemnity owed by commercial distributors, a choice of law) the GPL cannot take. JPEG, WebP and TGA still need it.

